--TEST--
`put`'s trailing `{ttl?: Duration}` is a lifetime the entry does not outlive, and the tiers that hold their own entries answer it identically: an omitted `ttl` keeps § 1's contract, a lifetime still ahead reads back, and one already over leaves nothing under the key — `rule:core-api/shape-rules`, `rule:concurrency/cross-request-state-is-explicit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2's trailing options shape, and R12's unit as a
// type: the lifetime is a `Core\Time\Duration` literal, so no call site has to
// remember which scale this member chose.
var $local = Core\Cache::local();
var $process = Core\Cache::process();

// An omitted `ttl` is the contract every entry already had — present until the
// tier's cap forgets it. The option is optional here where
// `rule:core-api/a-lifetime-is-written` makes a signature's lifetime a required
// key, because nothing a cache holds leaves the process.
$local->put("no-lifetime", "kept");
$process->put("no-lifetime", "kept");

// A lifetime still ahead of every read below.
$local->put("ahead", "kept", {ttl: 1h});
$process->put("ahead", "kept", {ttl: 1h});

// Asked of both tiers and counted rather than read off a line: a tier that
// answered plausibly for the omitted lifetime and wrongly for the written one
// would still print a row of its own.
array<string> $inside = ["no-lifetime", "ahead"];
int $present = 0;
string $back = "";
foreach ($inside as string $key) {
    $back = ($local->get($key) ?? "") as string;
    if ($back == "kept") {
        $present = $present + 1;
    }
    $back = ($process->get($key) ?? "") as string;
    if ($back == "kept") {
        $present = $present + 1;
    }
}
echo "inside its lifetime: ", $present, " of ", Core\Arr::count($inside) * 2, "\n";

// The other side of the same bound. A lifetime that has already run out is not
// a write at all: `put` still cannot fail — § 1 gives this store no failure
// mode to reach for — and the key is left with nothing under it.
$local->put("over", "gone", {ttl: 0s});
$process->put("over", "gone", {ttl: 0s});
echo "past it, the local tier: ", $local->get("over") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";
echo "past it, the process tier: ", $process->get("over") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";

// And an entry that was really there goes with it, because a `put` replaced it
// — the same exit an arrival too large for the tier takes.
$local->put("replaced", "was here");
$process->put("replaced", "was here");
$local->put("replaced", "and now not", {ttl: 0s});
$process->put("replaced", "and now not", {ttl: 0s});
var $both = $local->get("replaced") == null && $process->get("replaced") == null;
echo "a lifetime already over replaces nothing: ", $both ? "both null" : "one kept it", "\n";

// The third tier takes the same option to a store that keeps its own clock, so
// the lifetime never crosses as an instant. A deployment that configured none
// is told so by name rather than handed the per-core store under it.
try {
    Core\Cache::shared();
    echo "shared handed back a store\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo "the shared tier keeps its own clock, and this deployment has no store\n";
}
--EXPECT--
inside its lifetime: 4 of 4
past it, the local tier: null
past it, the process tier: null
a lifetime already over replaces nothing: both null
the shared tier keeps its own clock, and this deployment has no store
